    4. Derive the 4 quadrant masks from the output by reversing the overlay.
       For each cell, whichever color c is in the output, mark that cell as foreground
       in the quadrant that owns color c. For cells where a higher-priority quadrant
       already claims the pixel, lower-priority quadrants may independently have fg or bg
       at that position -- randomly choose (bias ~40-60% fg) to create realistic overlap.
    5. Assemble input as (2Q+1)x(2Q+1) grid -- place 4 quadrants around the separator cross.
    6. Validate -- each quadrant has exactly 1 fg color, 4-11 fg pixels, cross is solid.
